  text), so you write that code either way. You're choosing whether to add three lines on top of it, not choosing
  between two implementations.

  [1mShort design (if you take the recommendation)[0m

  [1mAPI[0m — one numeric option rather than a boolean plus a length, since neither is meaningful alone:

  [33mformat[39m([31m"The quick brown fox"[39m, { [36mtruncate[39m: [32m12[39m })  [32m// "The quick..."[39m

  [1mPlacement[0m — applied to [38;5;153mresult[39m after the case transforms, before [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m, following the existing sequential-guard
  style in [38;5;153mformat.js:2[39m.

  [1mThree decisions I'm assuming — say if you'd flip any:[0m
  1. [38;5;153mtruncate[39m bounds the content [3mincluding[0m the [38;5;153m'...'[39m, so output never exceeds the number you pass.
  2. It bounds the content, not the final string — an explicit [38;5;153mprefix[39m/[38;5;153msuffix[39m is caller-controlled and still appended
     after.
  3. Trailing whitespace is trimmed before the ellipsis, so you get [38;5;153m"The quick..."[39m not [38;5;153m"The quick ..."[39m.

  [1mEdge cases:[0m string already within the limit returns unchanged with no ellipsis; [38;5;153mtruncate <= 3[39m returns just the
  ellipsis clipped to that length; no space inside the budget falls back to the hard cut.

